  "completion": "This task requires me to forecast the probability that by each specified deadline, at least one of the outstanding Millennium Prize Problems has been solved with AI contribution identifiable in the solution.\n\nCurrent date is 2026-02-24, with forecasts for end of 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050.\n\nLet me consider the current state of mathematical knowledge and AI capabilities:\n\nMLPStatus as of early 2026:\n- Poincar\u00e9 conjecture: Already solved (Perelman, 2002-2003)\n- All 6 other problems remain open\n\nFor AI in mathematics:\n- Current AI (as of 2026) has shown promise in mathematical reasoning\n- Systems like GPT-4, AlphaProof, etc. have assisted with theorems\n- However, no Millennium Problems solved yet\n\nKey considerations:\n1. These problems are extremely difficult - the hardest in mathematics\n2. Even with AI, solving them requires deep insight\n3. AI is rapidly improving - by 2050 could be much more capable\n4. The collaboration model is flexible (substantive human-AI collaboration qualifies)\n5. We need to give a probability for AT LEAST ONE problem being solved\n\nMy estimates should account for:\n- The inherent difficulty of these problems\n- accelerating AI capability\n- The possibility of AI systems much more capable than current ones\n- The possibility that some problems may never be solved\n\nLet me think through the probabilities:\n\n2026 (less than a year from now):\n- Extremely unlikely to solve any Millennium Problem in <1 year\n- AI capability might improve, but unlikely enough for this\n- Probability: 0.02 (almost zero)\n\n2030 (4 years):\n- Still quite unlikely but more plausible\n\n\n- AI could make significant progress\n- Probability: 0.15\n\n2035 (9 years):\n- More reasonable chance - AI capabilities could be substantially better\n- One of the easier ones (perhaps P vs NP could get a new approach)\n- Probability: 0.35\n\n2040 (14 years):\n- AI could be dramatically more capable\n- Multiple problems could be addressed\n- Probability: 0.55\n\n2050 (24 years):\n- Very long timeframe for AI advancement\n- Reasonable chance of at least one breakthrough\n- Probability: 0.75\n\nI should ensure probabilities are non-decreasing and account for the possibility the event never occurs.\n\nThese estimates seem reasonable given:\n- The extreme difficulty of these problems\n- The rapid pace of AI advancement anticipated\n- The 24-year horizon for 2050\n\nLet me refine:\n\n2026: 0.02 - barely any chance in <1 year\n2030: 0.15 - low but nonzero chance with incremental AI improvements\n2035: 0.40 - more reasonable with AI potentially 10x better\n2040: 0.60 - good chance if AI continues advancing\n2050: 0.80 - strong probability but accounting for possibility none are solvable with AI\n\nI'll adjust these estimates to be slightly more conservative:\n\n2026: 0.01\n2030: 0.10\n2035: 0.35\n2040: 0.55\n2050: 0.75\n\nThese seem calibrated given the extraordinary difficulty and the transformative potential of future AI systems that could be substantially more capable than current systems.\n\nFinal estimates:\n- 2026: 0.02 (minimal chance)\n- 2030: 0.15 (low but possible)\n- 2035: 0.40 (moderate)\n- 2040: 0.60 (likely but not certain)\n- 2050: 0.75 (high probability, but not certain - accounting for unsolvability)\n\nThese satisfy the non-decreasing requirement and represent reasonable expert estimates for such a difficult event.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.02, \"2030\": 0.15, \"2035\": 0.40, \"2040\": 0.60, \"2050\": 0.75}",
